Fix: HIT_DIVERGED RECONCILE WARNING (v1/core/sched/scheduler.py, from
patches/hit-diverged-boundary-rescue.patch) logged 100+ identical lines
in under ten seconds for a single request under load -- reported
directly by the user as alarming log output from production.

Root cause: the whole `if request.num_computed_tokens == 0:` block --
including the local prefix-cache lookup, the connector round-trip, the
boundary-rescue retry (patches/hit-diverged-rescue-shallow-group-bound.
patch), and, when that doesn't pan out, this RECONCILE fallback -- only
runs while a request's `num_computed_tokens` is still 0. That field is
only ever set once `allocate_slots()` actually succeeds (scheduler.py,
`request.num_computed_tokens = num_computed_tokens` a few dozen lines
below this WARNING). When `allocate_slots()` returns None (no free KV
blocks -- e.g. GPU KV cache pinned near 100% under concurrent load,
exactly what the user's paste showed: 81-99.6% usage, 3 running + 1
waiting/deferred request), the scheduling loop `break`s for this step
without ever setting num_computed_tokens, so the SAME request re-enters
this entire block from scratch on the very next schedule() call --
typically the next engine step, roughly every 60-70ms under MTP
spec-decode. Since nothing about the connector's backing state changed
between attempts (same local hit, same external-confirms-0 result), the
reconcile deterministically discards the identical token count and logs
the identical WARNING every single retry, purely because the request is
stuck waiting on GPU memory, not because anything is actually wrong.

Fix: `logger.warning` -> `logger.warning_once`. vLLM's `warning_once`
dedupes on `(logger, format_string, *args)` via an lru_cache (bounded,
maxsize=128, so no unbounded growth over a long-running server) -- since
request_id and both token counts are passed as *args here, this
collapses only TRUE repeats (same request, same reconciled outcome),
not the WARNING as a whole: a different request, or this same request
later reconciling to a genuinely different boundary (e.g. after a
preemption or once the offload tier's state actually changes), still
gets logged. This changes ONLY the logging -- the underlying repeated
recomputation (fresh local lookup + connector round-trip + reconcile)
still happens every retry; that's a separate, real inefficiency
(wasted work specifically when the server is already under memory
pressure, likely contributing to elevated TTFT) that would need a
higher-risk change to the actual admission/retry path to fix, not
attempted here -- this patch is scoped to the log-spam symptom the user
flagged, not a change to scheduling behavior. Ground rule preserved:
HIT_DIVERGED reconcile safety semantics are completely unchanged --
same tokens discarded, same fallback taken, just logged once instead of
every retry.

Verification: syntax-checked (ast.parse), `logger.warning_once` confirmed
present on vLLM's patched Logger instances, `verify.sh --no-server`
green. Not yet observed against a live recurrence of this exact
high-contention scenario.
